The Rust binding gives you typed `Runtime`, `Session`, `Reference`, and
`Argument` over the glowcap archives
([`lib.rs`](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/bindings/rust/src/lib.rs)).
The round-trip below leads with iroh. Enable the `iroh` Cargo feature for that
tab. Start from the
[checkout instructions]($link.page('bindings')).

# The same round-trip

This is the [Start building]($link.page('start')) round-trip in Rust. It uses
the crate's opt-in `async` feature inside a `tokio::task::LocalSet`. `Runtime`
starts the inbound loop itself, so `serve` is only for awaiting shutdown.

```=html
<!-- site-examples:dial:rust GENERATED by `zig build gen-site-examples`; edits are overwritten -->
```
> []($block.attrs('code-tabs'))
> **iroh**
> ```rust
> use glowcap::iroh::{self, Reachability};
> use glowcap::{Argument, ConnectOptions, PendingWork, Runtime, RuntimeConfig};
>
> // Discovery resolves a bare iroh node ID without an address hint.
> // Cargo.toml: glowcap = { …, features = ["iroh"] }
> let node_id = "<64 lowercase hex characters from the server>";
> let partner = iroh::locator(node_id, None);
>
> let runtime = Runtime::open(RuntimeConfig::providers([iroh::provider(iroh::Config::new(
>     Reachability::Discovery,
> ))]))
> .await?;
> ```
> **webrtc**
> ```rust
> use std::rc::Rc;
>
> use glowcap::webrtc::{self, SignalingAuthority};
> use glowcap::{Argument, ConnectOptions, PendingWork, Runtime, RuntimeConfig};
>
> // Your signaling carries the offer and each candidate to the peer the locator
> // names, over a path you already hold, such as an OCapN capability both peers
> // can reach, and brings the answer and the peer's candidates back. Drive the
> // runtime from a tokio `LocalSet`: the callbacks run on the thread that steps it.
> // Cargo.toml: glowcap = { …, features = ["webrtc"] }
> let signaling: Rc<dyn SignalingAuthority> = todo!("your SignalingAuthority");
> let partner = webrtc::locator("<64 lowercase hex characters from the peer>");
>
> let runtime =
>     Runtime::open(RuntimeConfig::providers([webrtc::provider(signaling, webrtc::Config::default())])).await?;
> ```
> **noise-tcp**
> ```rust
> use glowcap::{tcp, Argument, ConnectOptions, PendingWork, Runtime, RuntimeConfig};
>
> // Same TCP sockets as keyed dialing; Noise_XK seals the CapTP stream.
> let peer_key = "<64 lowercase hex characters from the server>";
> let partner = tcp::noise::locator(peer_key, "127.0.0.1", 9099);
>
> let runtime = Runtime::open(RuntimeConfig::providers([tcp::provider(tcp::Config::default())])).await?;
> ```
> **noise-relay**
> ```rust
> use glowcap::{relay, Argument, ConnectOptions, PendingWork, Runtime, RuntimeConfig};
>
> // Noise_XK seals the CapTP stream end to end through the relay.
> let peer_key = "<64 lowercase hex characters from the server>";
> let relay_url = "wss://relay.example/relay";
> let partner = relay::noise::locator(peer_key, relay_url);
> let runtime = Runtime::open(RuntimeConfig::providers([relay::provider(
> 	relay::Config::dial_only(relay_url),
> )]))
> .await?;
> ```
> **onion**
> ```rust
> use glowcap::onion::{self, Config};
> use glowcap::{Argument, ConnectOptions, PendingWork, Runtime, RuntimeConfig};
>
> // glowcap starts no Tor; point the provider at an existing SOCKS endpoint.
> let partner = onion::locator("<56-character onion service id>");
> let runtime = Runtime::open(RuntimeConfig::providers([onion::provider(
> 	Config::dial_only("/run/tor/socks"),
> )]))
> .await?;
> ```
> **keyed-tcp**
> ```rust
> use glowcap::{tcp, Argument, ConnectOptions, PendingWork, Runtime, RuntimeConfig};
>
> // Key designator, cleartext CapTP. Partners dial the public key; the host:port is a hint.
> let peer_key = "<64 lowercase hex characters from the server>";
> let partner = tcp::keyed::locator(peer_key, "127.0.0.1", 9099);
>
> let runtime = Runtime::open(RuntimeConfig::providers([tcp::provider(tcp::Config::default())])).await?;
> ```
> **keyed-relay**
> ```rust
> use glowcap::{relay, Argument, ConnectOptions, PendingWork, Runtime, RuntimeConfig};
>
> // The key names the peer; the hint names the relay that can reach it.
> let peer_key = "<64 lowercase hex characters from the server>";
> let relay_url = "wss://relay.example/relay";
> let partner = relay::keyed::locator(peer_key, relay_url);
> let runtime = Runtime::open(RuntimeConfig::providers([relay::provider(
> 	relay::Config::dial_only(relay_url),
> )]))
> .await?;
> ```
> **tcp**
> ```rust
> use glowcap::{tcp, Argument, ConnectOptions, PendingWork, Runtime, RuntimeConfig};
>
> // Plain TCP uses a cleartext host and port.
> let partner = tcp::locator("127.0.0.1", 9099);
>
> let runtime = Runtime::open(RuntimeConfig::providers([tcp::provider(tcp::Config::default())])).await?;
> ```

From here, the CapTP workflow is the same for every transport:

```rust
let session = runtime.connect(&partner, ConnectOptions::default()).await?;
let greeter = session.fetch("greeter").await?; // a capability this peer published
match session.send(&greeter, "greet", &["world".into()]).await {
    Ok(Argument::String(reply)) => println!("{reply}"),
    Ok(other) => println!("unexpected reply: {other:?}"),
    Err(reason) => eprintln!("the greeter refused: {reason:?}"),
}
session.close(PendingWork::Cancel).await?;
runtime.close(PendingWork::Cancel).await?;
```
```=html
<!-- /site-examples:dial:rust -->
```

The CapTP verbs stay the same across tabs. What changes is the locator helper
and whether CapTP rides cleartext or Noise. The iroh tab needs the `iroh` Cargo
feature and a discovery-reachable node id.

# Hosting the other half

`listen` binds a `Server` whose `accept` yields a `Session` per partner. Keyed
and Noise listeners take a 32-byte seed so the advertised designator survives a
restart. iroh binds a node id instead of a socket address.

```=html
<!-- site-examples:listen:rust GENERATED by `zig build gen-site-examples`; edits are overwritten -->
```
> []($block.attrs('code-tabs'))
> **iroh**
> ```rust
> use glowcap::iroh::{self, Reachability};
> use glowcap::Argument;
>
> // This listener is named by its node id. Partners can dial it from anywhere.
> // Cargo.toml: glowcap = { …, features = ["iroh"] }
> let server = iroh::listen(
>     iroh::Config::new(Reachability::Discovery),
>     iroh::ListenOptions::default(),
> )
> .await?;
> ```
> **noise-tcp**
> ```rust
> use glowcap::{tcp, Argument};
>
> // Same bind as keyed-tcp; Noise_XK seals every accepted CapTP stream.
> let seed = [7u8; 32]; // replace with durable secret bytes across restarts
> let server = tcp::noise::listen(
>     "127.0.0.1",
>     9099,
>     tcp::ListenOptions {
>         identity: tcp::ListenerIdentity::Seed(seed),
>         ..tcp::ListenOptions::default()
>     },
> )
> .await?;
> ```
> **noise-relay**
> ```rust
> use glowcap::{relay, Argument};
>
> // Register this key at the relay; Noise_XK seals every accepted CapTP stream.
> let seed = [7u8; 32]; // replace with durable secret bytes across restarts
> let server = relay::noise::listen(
>     "wss://relay.example/relay",
>     "wss://relay.example/register",
>     relay::ListenOptions {
>         identity: relay::ListenerIdentity::Seed(seed),
>         ..relay::ListenOptions::default()
>     },
> )
> .await?;
> ```
> **onion**
> ```rust
> use glowcap::onion::{self, Auth, Config};
> use glowcap::Argument;
>
> // glowcap starts no Tor. Name an already-running daemon's SOCKS (and control to host).
> let config = Config::dial_only("/run/tor/socks")
>     .with_control("/run/tor/control", Auth::None);
> let server = onion::listen(config, onion::ListenOptions::default()).await?;
> let service_key = server.service_key(); // persist securely to restore this service
> ```
> **keyed-tcp**
> ```rust
> use glowcap::{tcp, Argument};
>
> // Key designator, cleartext CapTP. Reuse a 32-byte seed to keep the same key.
> let seed = [7u8; 32]; // replace with durable secret bytes across restarts
> let server = tcp::keyed::listen(
>     "127.0.0.1",
>     9099,
>     tcp::ListenOptions {
>         identity: tcp::ListenerIdentity::Seed(seed),
>         ..tcp::ListenOptions::default()
>     },
> )
> .await?;
> ```
> **tcp**
> ```rust
> use glowcap::{tcp, Argument};
>
> // Plain TCP binds the host and port you choose.
> let server = tcp::listen("127.0.0.1", 9099, tcp::ListenOptions::default()).await?;
> ```

From here, the CapTP workflow is the same for every transport:

```rust
println!("{}", server.uri()?); // hand this locator to partners
server.host().publish("greeter", || Box::new(greeter))?;
loop {
    let session = server.accept().await?;
    session.serve().await?;
}

fn greeter(verb: &str, args: &[Argument]) -> Result<Argument, String> {
    if verb != "greet" {
        return Err("no such method".into());
    }
    let Some(Argument::String(name)) = args.first() else {
        return Err("bad invocation".into());
    };
    Ok(Argument::String(format!("Hello, {name}").into()))
}
```
```=html
<!-- /site-examples:listen:rust -->
```

# Async

Async rides [tokio](https://tokio.rs/) behind an opt-in `async` feature. Handles
are `!Send`, so each lives on one task inside a `LocalSet`. Only the socket reads and writes await. The protocol verbs never block.
`session.serve().await` waits until that session shuts down, and dropping the
`Session` closes the connection.

# Actors

`session.spawn` and `session.publish` take a `FnMut(&str, &[Argument])` closure
returning `Result<Argument, String>`. An `Err` breaks the caller's promise with its message. A panic breaks it too and never unwinds into the C library.
To let a third peer reach an actor directly through an introduction, call
`publish` on the `Host` from `server.host()`.
[Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) covers pipelining and attenuation.

The complete API lives in the
[Rust binding README](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/bindings/rust/README.md),
and the
[rooms chat client](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/apps/rooms/clients/rust/client.rs)
shows the binding in a complete application.
